      "text": "```python\n\"\"\"\nLeast Recently Used (LRU) Cache Implementation\n\nAn LRU cache is a fixed-size cache that evicts the least recently used items\nwhen the cache is full. It provides O(1) time complexity for both get and put\noperations by combining a hash map (dictionary) with a doubly linked list.\n\nHow it works:\n- The hash map stores key-value pairs for O(1) lookup.\n- The doubly linked list maintains the order of usage, with the most recently\n  used item at the head and the least recently used item at the tail.\n- When an item is accessed (get) or inserted/updated (put), it is moved to\n  the head of the list (most recently used).\n- When the cache exceeds its capacity, the item at the tail (least recently\n  used) is evicted.\n\nTime Complexity:\n- get(key): O(1) average case\n- put(key, value): O(1) average case\n- Space Complexity: O(capacity)\n\nExample:\n    >>> cache = LRUCache(2)\n    >>> cache.put(1, 1)\n    >>> cache.put(2, 2)\n    >>>",
